        <body>
            <div xml:id="d402">

                <pb facs="0511" n="391" sameAs="frus:frus1931v02/pg391"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">368.117/286: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chargé in Greece</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Fisher</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-09-30T16:00:00-05:00">September 30, 1931—4
                        p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>30. Your 1682 of April 3, 1931 regarding exemption from alleged military
                    obligations. The Department must now order its supply of Notices to Bearers of
                    Passports, for use in 1932. The text of the revised edition must be ready for
                    the printer by the middle of October.</p>
                <p>The question therefore arises at this time as to what advice should be given in
                    the new publication to naturalized American citizens of Greek origin who propose
                    to visit Greece during 1932.</p>
                <p>It is suggested that you bring this matter to the personal attention of the
                    Foreign Minister as urgently as possible, expressing the hope of this Government
                    that the present favorable arrangement may be continued during the coming
                    year.</p>
                <p>You may add that the Department has given appropriate publicity to the liberal
                    attitude adopted by the Greek Government with respect to the present year.</p>
                <p>Please reply by telegram before October 15.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
